00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.310 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  for many years I was focused on health  policy and I actually worked with the  Clinton administration to try to pass  health reform and when a colleague of  mine came and said you know what's the  next important thing you want to work on  what are the big problems I said you  know clearly care coordination is the  big problem and so I took it on and  started doing work around it and you  know like everybody else I came across  my own you know horror story really my  father got ill he spent more time than  we would have liked in the medical care  system going from Doctor to doctor going  from hospital to hospital my siblings  and I did our very best to keep a  notebook with all the notes and we would  transmit the information as best we  could from Doctor to doctor and you know  ultimately I say that my father passed  away because of a care coordination  failure so the the problem that I care a  lot about and have focused a great deal  of my research on recently has been care  coordination I call it integrated care  the reason I call it integrated care is  because I feel like it goes beyond just  mere coordination I really feel like  integration is something more holistic  because it accounts for all of the  customization that's required to care  for a particular patient so it is the  communication and it is the coordination  but it's also it's done on behalf of  each individual patient according to the  patient's needs  traditionally the system has grown up in  silo so doctors have trained alone  nurses have chained separately they're  trained to optimize what it is they do  not so much to communicate with each  other and that's it's the between the  cracks where the problems occur so  information doesn't transmit when  information doesn't transmit you know if  a patient's been in a hospital and then  they go home if that information about  the hospitalization doesn't get back to  the primary care provider that patient  is at risk for not having a follow-up  appointment is much more likely to get  readmitted back to the hospital if a  primary care physician refers a patient  out for a screening exam but doesn't get  the information about the result of that  exam back that patient is at risk for  not having the appropriate follow-up  we have a traditional payment system in  the US where predominantly providers are  still paid on a fee-for-service basis  that means if they perform a service  they get a fee for that service that  encourages providers to provide more  services it does not encourage providers  to spend time coordinating care on  behalf of their patients and so there  are efforts to reform the payment  systems in ways that align the  incentives with the kinds of initiatives  that I've been talking about so rather  than a strict fee-for-service there are  experiments around for example  accountable care organizations and some  accountable care organizations assume  risk for either physician services or  for all services and you have more of an  incentive to try and create the kinds of  processes and programs that I've been  describing that allow the providers to  attend to the coordination needs of  those patients  for decades the VA has been a leader in  terms of quality and efficiency of the  care that they deliver on behalf of the  veterans and the reason that they have  been so far ahead of the rest of the  system in the u.s. is because it's an  integrated delivery system it's kind of  one of the very few we have examples of  in the United States the primary doctors  and the specialists and the hospitals  they're all part of the same system and  they have for decades shared an  electronic medical record system which  means that as a primary care doctor I  could see what the specialist has done  on behalf of my patient and I can follow  up in a way that in the private health  care system we very often don't have the  ability to do so for those reasons they  have had great advantages to be able to  deliver high quality and more integrated  care and they have had their share of  visionary leaders who've really  prioritized quality and and coordination  and patient centeredness and when you  have that along with caring for your  patient you know the combination of  those things are pretty powerful  everybody really wants to do their best  they're instituting a new program called  their care coordination and integrated  case management program they're creating  hierarchical levels of interventions so  for their very most complex patients  they have one set of processes so they  are actually assigning a care  coordinator to work with that patient  and the family to go with that patient  to their different visits and to make  sure that the information flows back to  their primary care doctor and for the  next swath of patients still complex but  a little bit less so they have another  level of intervention and then for a  level down from there a third level of  intervention so they're trying to assign  the resources according to the needs of  the patients  we've gone into practice sites to try to  understand what they're doing what tools  they're using to try to tease out what  what's a better practice then then not a  better practice and interestingly when  we did this initially over 150 practice  sites we couldn't discern anything that  was differentiating them the things that  we expected would be differentiating  whether they had a shared electronic  medical record system whether they were  multidisciplinary whether they were  sophisticated in terms of their IT or  sophisticated in terms of their care  management processes none of those  things systematically related to  improving integration for their patients  and so we went to organizations that the  patient's suggested were high performing  versus low performing to try to tease  out some of the differences and what we  finally discerned kind of at its essence  was that organizations could take  whatever resources they had available  whether it be a sophisticated electronic  medical record system or whether it was  a kind of individual resource like a  care management person within the  organization so long as they took those  resources and really focus their  attention on on the goal which was to  integrate care for the patients there  were lots of different paths to getting  there  if somebody says I want to start where  should I start I always tell them to  measure first to find out where their  unique strengths are and where their  unique challenges are and once they have  that they can really create for  themselves a road map that I think makes  sense because every organization is  going to be a little bit different that  way  what we're looking at is is trying to  get people to make incremental  improvement there's a tremendous amount  of focus on this country in the next  great you know drug or the next great  device and instead my research focus is  on kind of the next great improvement in  ways that we can transmit information  and coordinate care because if we don't  do that then the you know the the drug  is you know not going to produce the  benefit that we need it to produce if we  have a procedure you know because it's a  great new procedure that we're able to  do with some sort of automated robotic  way of doing it you know that's  tremendous but if there is an  appropriate follow-up for that patient  they're not going to benefit from having  that procedure and they can end up  either worse off or back into the  hospital and so in order to make them  work well we need cultures within  organizations and people with any  organizations that prioritize the their  combination so it's going to be very  incremental but if we could actually  move the ball down the field we'd be  saving you know life after life after  life it would be a game-changer  [Music]  you

